    candidate for a hybrid one — and link it to this session. One body for the
    two things that build a child: the invocation starting it, and a restore
    resuming it where it stood (`do_resume_child`).

    The child takes the host its parent's host answers for it, the buffer its
    `#_parent` events wait in, the id it is started under (`started_under`, an
    expression: the start's `pending.invoke_id`, or the id a saved state
    carries) and its session id, `<this session>.<started under>`. Binds
    `child_policy` and `child_session_id`.
#}
{% macro build_child_policy(child, child_mod, child_policy_type, started_under) %}
                // W3C SCXML 6.5: Generate child session ID for finalize origin matching
                let child_session_id = format!("{}.{}",
                    self.session_id.as_deref().unwrap_or(""),
                    {{ started_under }});

                // W3C SCXML 6.4: Create child state machine
                // Engine DI Parity RFC: forward parent's script engine to child when the
                // child Policy was generated with `model.needs_script_engine = true`.
                //
                // W3C SCXML C.1: the child is built under the id its parent
                // recorded for it. The parent mints `parentSession.invokeId` and
                // keys `active_invokes` on it, so an event from this child
                // reaches the parent carrying that id as its origin; had the
                // child an id of its own, the location it publishes in
                // `_ioprocessors` and the origin the parent sees would be two
                // names for one session that can never be compared — which is
                // exactly what C.1 requires of them. A child's id comes from the
                // tree alone, and nothing is drawn for it (docs/adr/0045).
{% if child.child_needs_script_engine %}
                let mut child_policy = super::{{ child_mod }}::{{ child_policy_type }}Policy::for_child(child_session_id.clone(), {% if child.child_host_call %}{{ child.child_host_call }}, {% endif %}self.script_engine.clone());
{% else %}
                let mut child_policy = super::{{ child_mod }}::{{ child_policy_type }}Policy::for_child(child_session_id.clone(){% if child.child_host_call %}, {{ child.child_host_call }}{% endif %});
{% endif %}
                // W3C SCXML 6.4: Pass parent session info to child for #_parent routing
                child_policy.parent_external_queue = Some(engine.get_external_queue_handle());
                child_policy.invoke_id = {{ started_under }}.clone();
                child_policy.child_session_id = child_session_id.clone();
{% endmacro %}
{# §scxml-6.5: the `<finalize>` body of the static invoke `invoke_info`, as
   the expression `track_child` stores. It is AUTHORED content and reaches the
   engine like every other authored expression — through the ECMAScript
   frontend. The parser hands it over as JavaScript
   (`actions_to_javascript`), and this line used to escape that text and
   pass it to a Lua engine unlowered, so a `<finalize>` worked only when
   its JavaScript happened to be valid Lua as well. W3C test233's body is
   one bare assignment (`Var1 = _event.data.aParam`), which is valid in
   both, and it is the only `<finalize>` document with executable content
   in the whole corpus — so the surface passed on a coincidence.
   The C++ Interpreter never had the gap: it keeps the finalize as XML and
   re-parses it as executable content, which lowers each action normally. #}
{% macro static_finalize(invoke_info) -%}
